-- Issue-stage events, relative to the issue stage's scope (TOP.NzeaCore.core.middle.isu).
--
-- These are the events the retire counters cannot answer: the counters say how many, these say when and in what order.
-- The two stage registers are the middle's — declared one level up, beside every other stage boundary — so what is
-- watched here is this unit's end of them: the payload it reads (`decoded_*`) and the uop it hands to the register
-- below (`moved_*`). The slot those uops land in is the execute stage's read side, so its events live beside it
-- (`nzea_core/src/middle/exu/uop.pulse`), and the write-back stage's live beside it
-- (`nzea_core/src/middle/wbu/retire.pulse`); bind them together for the redirect each issued uop turns into (the
-- window is the read/write-back distance, so it is not a fixed number):
--   pulse property --event nzea_core/src/middle/isu/issue.pulse <ISU> Issue \
--       --event nzea_core/src/middle/exu/uop.pulse <EXU> Uop \
--       --event nzea_core/src/middle/wbu/retire.pulse <WB> Wbu \
--       --eval 'Uop.uop_issue -> Wbu.redirect'

-- There is a payload in the issue stage: what the read presents, or what it still waits for.
inflight = decoded_valid

-- The payload holds although the register below reports room: what stops it is its own operands — it reads the very
-- instruction that is finishing below it, and the write it waits for lands a cycle later. `moved_ready` is the
-- register's accept side, so it is the room; while `decoded_valid` holds and the room is there, the payload's own pace
-- is where the operand check lives.
operand_wait = decoded_valid && moved_ready && !decoded_ready

-- The read places its operands: the uop moves into the execute slot this cycle, and the slot presents it the next one.
uop_move = moved_valid && moved_ready
